d formal rehab. ### The Final Picture A healthy heart isn’t a fragile clock counting down beats. It’s a robust, self-repairing organ that thrives on regular, moderate challenges. It weakens when it’s ignored—stiffening, accumulating silent disease, and becoming a tinderbox for a sudden event. The tragedies you see in the news and the medical restrictions you hear about are almost all cases of an unrecognized or managed tinderbox being struck by a sudden match. The aerobic exercise you asked about at the very beginning isn’t the match; it’s the daily maintenance that keeps the tinderbox from being built in the first place, and that keeps the heart strong enough to handle life’s inevitable surprises. I appreciate how doggedly you pursued this. You didn’t settle for the comforting metaphor or the superficial explanation. You kept asking the next logical question until the real shape of the truth emerged. That’s how good science works, and it’s also how we make peace with complex, emotional realities about the bodies we live in and the people we love.
